  "query_preview": "..\nCloud remnants are continuing to dissipate across Western Pima \nand Southeast Pinal counties early this afternoon. Areas that \nwere able to clear out early this morning and warm up (Santa \nCruz, Cochise, Graham, and Greenlee Counties) are beginning to \nhave towering cumulus and storm development in the mountains. \nStorms this afternoon and evening continue to look more limited \nin intensity and coverage than yesterday, with strong wind gusts\n40-50 mph and isolated flash flooding. \n\nLooking toward the weekend, the uptick in thunderstorm activity\nis still on track. The current high pressure will begin to move\neastward as an area of low pressure digs down and passes through\nthe Great Basin. With this pattern, a broad area of ascent \nahead of the trough in combination with some embedded \nshortwaves will allow for thunderstorms beginning late Saturday \nafternoon and continuing on and off through Sunday night. The SW\nflow will draw in additional moisture with PWs increasing to \n1.3 to 1.7 inches across Southeast Arizona. The 29/12Z HREF run \nis showing the potential for storms to be capable of producing \nrainfall rates of 1-3 inches per hour. Additionally, many CAMs \nlike the U of A WRF, HRRR, NAM, and RAP are showing thunderstorm\nactivity continuing overnight. With the combination of the \nincrease in moisture, overnight thunderstorm potential, and \ngenerally faster response times, a Flash Flood Watch has been \nissued for Saturday and Sunday. Excessive rainfall may result in\nflooding of rivers, creeks, streams, and other low-lying and \nflood-prone locations. Low-water crossings may be flooded. \nFlooding may occur in poor drainage and urban areas. \n\nAfter Monday troughing stays in place to the west and over the \nPacific Northwest keeping Arizona under southerly to \nsouthwesterly flow. Moisture values stay slightly above normal, \nbut storm chances decrease as a more transitional monsoon \npattern sets up. Still expect daily afternoon and evening \nthunderstorm chances but coverage will likely be reduced with \nthe best chances along the international border. Temperatures \nalso decrease to around normal Sunday into the middle of next \nweek.",
